imes.com/photo/135912391.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/cctv-captures-let-commander-lateef-bhats-movements-before-kulgam-crpf-attack/articleshow/135912391.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/cctv-captures-let-commander-lateef-bhats-movements-before-kulgam-crpf-attack/articleshow/135912391.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-17T11:19:33+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Vivek Dubey</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135912505,imgsize-7382451.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[NMC asks all government medical colleges, attached hospitals to join road accident cashless treatment scheme]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/nmc-asks-all-government-medical-colleges-attached-hospitals-to-join-road-accident-cashless-treatment-scheme/articleshow/135912109.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135912109.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[National Medical Commission has directed government medical colleges to join the PM-RAHAT scheme for cashless treatment. This scheme aims to provide timely medical care for road accident victims within the crucial golden hour. The initiative ensures that designated hospitals offer up to Rs 1.5 lakh in treatment for seven days post-accident. Hospitals need to meet specific requirements, including 24/7 emergency services and equipped facilities.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/nmc-asks-all-government-medical-colleges-attached-hospitals-to-join-road-accident-cashless-treatment-scheme/articleshow/135912109.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/nmc-asks-all-government-medical-colleges-attached-hospitals-to-join-road-accident-cashless-treatment-scheme/articleshow/135912109.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-17T11:04:54+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Anuja Jaiswal</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135912256,imgsize-70588.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Forged birth certificate case: SC rejects UP govt's plea against stay on Azam Khan's conviction]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/forged-birth-certificate-case-sc-rejects-up-govts-plea-against-stay-on-azam-khans-conviction/articleshow/135911846.cms"&gt;&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/135911846.cms" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;<![CDATA[The Supreme Court has upheld the stay on Azam Khan's conviction, dismissing the Uttar Pradesh government's appeal. This verdict offers a significant reprieve to Khan's wife, Tazeen Fatma, and his son, Abdullah Azam Khan, who had filed similar appeals. Previously, the Allahabad High Court stayed Khan's conviction in a forged birth certificate case, highlighting the intricate legal battle surrounding Khan and his family.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/forged-birth-certificate-case-sc-rejects-up-govts-plea-against-stay-on-azam-khans-conviction/articleshow/135911846.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/forged-birth-certificate-case-sc-rejects-up-govts-plea-against-stay-on-azam-khans-conviction/articleshow/135911846.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-11-17T10:55:05+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Ayush Pandey</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-135911908,imgsize-888462.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[India lost 1,765 elephants in 17 years, Karnataka 223]]></title><description>&lt;a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-lost-1765-elephants-in-17-years-karnataka-223/artic